## 3. Test Plan

### Execution and evidence ordering

1. Complete T-001 via the returned canonical preparation route after TP approval. Capture exact baseline and hashes; inspect worker ownership, assembler propagation and supported local host registration before editing. A dirty candidate implementation path requires explicit isolation/reconciliation rather than overwriting it.
2. T-002 to T-005 verify exact dependency resolution, resource build, default/cockpit inventory, owned assembly and scoped connection config. Do not install into plugin cache or silently edit global host configuration. Produce the reviewable entry before any activation step that needs host permission. If activation cannot be performed, preserve prepared output and identify the host gap.
3. T-006 is the early feasibility checkpoint. Record host version/build, Node/platform, bridge protocol, advertised serverTools/display modes, actual selected mode, exact server/UI/lock digests, named loaded connection, method responses and rendered observations. Synthetic protocol probes are labelled; they establish feasibility only. The final source/packet journey remains T-012.
4. T-007 to T-010 implement the selected product path with deterministic fixture/fake-clock/deferred-response tests where required to reproduce boundaries and races. Then T-011 runs consolidated compatibility and routed reviews. Do not equate scenario rows with test counts or weaken assertions to fit the implementation.
5. T-012 records the complete actual-host journey, failures, no-write window, rollback/reconnect and setup/knowledge evidence. Canonical QA determines pass/revise/block; human QA/UAT approvals and applicable OR remain separate. The TP predicts no successful outcome.

### Automated checks and planned test ownership

Use supported Node 22.22.3 already available, preserving project engine support. New behavioral tests belong beside existing owners: `packages/core/test/cockpit-context-test.js` for graph/packet/limits; `packages/cli/scripts/cockpit-mcp-test.js` for strict local mode/session/composition/provenance; existing `packages/mcp-server` tests for SDK registration/resource/tool wire behavior; existing private UI test lanes for shared transport, rendering, bridge controller and build. These are planned files/coverage, not commands already executed.

Run the resulting Core/CLI tests with Node; run `npm --prefix packages/mcp-server test`, `npm --prefix packages/control-ui run typecheck`, `npm --prefix packages/control-ui run build`, `npm --prefix packages/control-ui test` and `npm --prefix packages/control-ui run test:browser`. Add a named private MCP-app build script under the existing Vite owner; record its exact command/output and assembled resource digest. Protocol tests must cover supported negotiated protocol eras where SDK/host actually support them; do not claim unsupported-era compatibility from schema inspection.

Run affected existing regressions: `packages/core/test/control-state-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/control-command-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/control-inspect-test.js`, `packages/core/test/interaction-presentation-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/plugin-mcp-runtime-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/mcp-lifecycle-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/runtime-integrity-layout-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/runtime-integrity-negative-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/local-preparation-test.js`, `packages/cli/scripts/package-contents-test.js` and `packages/cli/scripts/payload-budget-test.js`. Also run assembler/local-development tests that own changed profile/preparation branches. Existing verification owners continue supplying required checks; no new parallel verification framework. Public/default inventory comparison is meaningful because UI dependencies must stay private. If assembly needs generated prerequisites, use the existing synchronization/preparation owner before testing; record exact output profile and prerequisite ordering.

Boundary tests use disposable controlled files/registered sources, not huge live repository captures. Fake clocks cover expiry without hours of waiting. Inject controlled source mutation/read/bridge responses for race reproduction, retaining at least one real stdio integration and real captured filesystem lane. Capture response/packet UTF-8 sizes using the same serialized form sent to the host. Source/schema/default-profile failure tests must prove the actual production boundary, not a test-only guard.

### Manual and actual-host checks

Use keyboard and pointer journeys at supported narrow/wide sizes and the actual native panel/display mode: compact entry, explicit run selection, source identity, passive artefact, maintained graph node, inclusion/exclusion, preparation, context acceptance and deliberate question. Custom follow-up uses Codex's existing composer. Verify visible context/source identity without claiming acknowledgement proves a correct answer. Preserve source/protocol, browser, actual-host and human-UAT results separately; fixture/browser successes cannot satisfy a missing actual-host criterion.

The no-write window hashes canonical control bytes immediately before and after app-only operations, including context and question. No agent control bookkeeping or graph curation occurs inside that window. Later evidence writes occur outside it and cannot be used to conceal an app mutation. A live later delivery request, if exercised, uses the installed independent connection and fresh canonical binding; never send an approval string or implementation request as the app's test question.

Evidence records scenario ID, exact fixture or project source identity, command/action, observed expected/actual outcome, time, runtime/UI digest, and any failure/uncertainty. Reports use bounded logs without session secrets. Test skips, inaccessible capabilities and stale earlier results remain gaps. Cooperative self-review is labelled and never described as independent review.

## 4. Brownfield Scope

Inspect and reuse `packages/core/lib/control-read/`, `control-inspect/cockpit.js`, run parser/evaluator and Core composition; `packages/control-ui/server/pool.mjs`, `read-worker.mjs`, HTTP/session wrappers, `src/App.tsx`, `state.ts`, `api.ts`, `types.ts`, existing views and Vite owner; `packages/mcp-server/bin/`, `src/` and its tests; `packages/cli/lib/mcp-dispatch-runtime.js`, local development preparation and `mcp-lifecycle/package.js`; `scripts/assemble-npm.mjs` and owned profile/package checks. The approved SD owns module boundaries and versions. Wrapper compatibility is verified rather than retaining a second worker implementation.

Only current-run control artefacts and necessary implementation/derived paths belong to this scope. Existing dirty MASTER_BACKLOG/current-run records are process state, not permission to alter unrelated runs. `.agdf/control/CONTEXT_GRAPH.md` remains maintained knowledge; curate verified implementation facts into existing appropriate nodes through closeout, preserving the open impact until evidence exists. Do not create graph relationships merely from planning. Installed AGDF/default runtime, protected host settings, unrelated projects and the existing browser process are not general repair targets.

## 5. Out Of Scope

Graph editor/database, inferred relevance, recursive context crawling, automatic completeness/Soll-Ist evaluation, generic filesystem/shell/network tools, gate/approval writers, automatic contextual questions, a second chat composer, persistent app selection/packet storage, remote/multiuser/public deployment, broad plugin/cache repair, new host adapters, public SDK/runtime migration, commit/push/PR/release/publication and transfer of prior browser-run approvals. No architecture/version fallback without the applicable source revision route.

## 6. Risks And Blockers

| Risk or trigger | Required response and evidence owner |
|---|---|
| Exact SDK packages unavailable, incompatible or contaminate the owned server graph | Stop dependency/build lane; record registry/build evidence and route SD revision before substitution; UI/MCP owner |
| Actual host cannot load named app, provide usable larger view or accept required context/message methods | T-006 blocks extensive dependent feature work; preserve precise feasibility gap and fallback, route design/evidence owner; never declare embedded completion from mocks |
| Protected configuration/registration workflow unavailable | Preserve ready reviewable entry and use supported authorized workflow only; external permission/state gap stays explicit, not a silently edited global config |
| Source/selector/provenance mismatch, mutation, limit or invalidation uncertainty | Reject/block affected handoff with exact reason; Core/UI owner fixes and reruns affected scenarios |
| Browser/default MCP or owned assembly regression | Block QA, repair within approved scope and rerun directly affected compatibility checks; scope growth routes back to earliest affected gate |
| Missing live-host/no-write/rollback/graph knowledge evidence | Keep criterion/closeout open; QA/OR owners decide readiness, no presumed acceptance |
| Historical model context persists after lost host/process or later source edit | Record bounded observation and limitation; disable uncertain new handoff and require fresh validation for later use, without claiming historical messages can be erased |

No debt waiver, authority shortcut or second source of truth is accepted. A material implementation discovery that changes approved scope/design goes through canonical revision rather than editing approved PRD/SD bytes.

## 7. Next Step

Review and approve only with `Approval: TP`. Valid approval permits the routed Brownfield Analysis and then scoped implementation once its prerequisites are satisfied. It does not approve QA/UAT/OR, protected host writes outside permission, version substitution or VCS/release actions.

